<LegalPage title="Privacy Notice" kicker="For everyone">
<p class="lede">The short version: we collect what we need to put art in front of people and to let people reach the artists who made it. We don’t sell it. We don’t run ads. And we don’t follow you around the internet.</p>

<h3>Who we are</h3>
<p>Artists Are Jerks is run by Alan Just Design, Alan Just’s sole proprietorship, in Medford, Oregon. You can reach us at info@artistsarejerks.com.</p>

<h3>If you’re just looking around</h3>
<p>You don’t need an account, and we don’t collect anything about you. Two things happen in the background that you should know about. Our host, Cloudflare, handles your network address to deliver pages and fend off attacks. And the site’s fonts come from Google Fonts, so Google sees that address too. If you tap “Directions,” you’re sent to Google Maps.</p>

<h3>If you write to an artist</h3>
<p>We keep your name, your email, and your message. We email it to the artist, and it also waits in their inbox on the site. The artist sees your email address so they can write back. It stays there until the artist deletes it, or for two years, whichever comes first.</p>

<h3>If you follow an artist</h3>
<p>We keep your email, which artist you follow, and when you confirmed. The artist can see the list and download it. Every email we send you has a link to stop. If you unsubscribe, we keep a note that you did, so you don’t get signed up again by accident.</p>

<h3>If you report a piece</h3>
<p>We keep the reason you picked, anything you wrote, and your email if you gave one. The artist doesn’t see who reported their work.</p>

<h3>If you send a copyright notice or counter-notice</h3>
<p>We keep it, along with the contact details in it, and the law expects us to share it with the other side: a notice goes to the artist who posted the work, and a counter-notice goes to the person who complained. See <a href="/copyright/">Copyright</a>.</p>

<h3>Spam protection on every form</h3>
<p>Cloudflare Turnstile checks that a real person is using the form, based on signals from your browser. To limit how often a form can be used, we store a scrambled, one-way code made from your network address. It can’t be turned back into the address, and we never store the address itself.</p>

<h3>If you’re an artist</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Your account:</strong> your name, email, and sign-in details, handled by Clerk, our sign-in service.</li>
  <li><strong>Your request:</strong> your answers and the photos you sent with them.</li>
  <li><strong>Your page:</strong> bio, city, pieces, prices, and showings. None of it is public until you’re approved and you publish. Even then, only pieces you mark to show are visible.</li>
  <li><strong>Contact details:</strong> your email and phone stay private unless you choose to show them.</li>
  <li><strong>Agreements:</strong> which version of these terms you agreed to, and when.</li>
</ul>

<h3>If you write to us from your workspace</h3>
<p>We keep your note, what it’s about, and the name and email on your account, so we can answer. Notes are deleted after two years.</p>

<h3>If you represent a venue</h3>
<p>We keep the venue’s name, address, hours, and description, plus a contact name, email, and phone. Venue details you mark public appear on the site. Your contact information doesn’t. We also keep which version of the Community Guidelines and Site Terms you agreed to, and when.</p>

<h3>What we do with it</h3>
<p>We use it to run the site, review requests, send the emails the site sends (approvals, forwarded messages, follow confirmations, showing notices, and notes about your account), stop spam and abuse, and keep a record of what people agreed to. That’s the list. No ads, no selling, no sharing for marketing, and no analytics trackers.</p>

<h3>Who helps us</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site, stores the database and images, sends the emails, and runs the spam check.</li>
  <li><strong>Clerk</strong> handles accounts and sign-in.</li>
  <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong> serves the typefaces.</li>
</ul>
<p>They handle information on our behalf, not for their own marketing. We’ll share information with law enforcement only when the law requires it, and we report any child sexual abuse material to the authorities.</p>

<h3>Cookies and browser storage</h3>
<p>If you sign in, Clerk sets cookies to keep you signed in. The spam check may use its own. The artist workspace keeps unsaved edits in your browser until they’re saved. There are no advertising or tracking cookies, which is why you won’t see a cookie banner.</p>

<h3>How long we keep things</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Artist and venue accounts: until you close them.</li>
  <li>Messages: until the artist deletes them, and never more than two years. Once a month, anything older is deleted.</li>
  <li>Follows: until you unsubscribe (plus the note that you did).</li>
  <li>Notes you send us: two years.</li>
  <li>Reports: one year after they’re closed.</li>
  <li>Backups: up to 30 days.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Your choices</h3>
<p>You can ask to see what we have about you, correct it, or delete it. Write to info@artistsarejerks.com. Artists can edit their page anytime. Some state privacy laws give you these rights formally. We’ll honor the request either way.</p>

<h3>Kids</h3>
<p>The site isn’t meant for children under 13, and we don’t knowingly collect their information. Artist accounts are for people 18 and up.</p>

<h3>Keeping it safe</h3>
<p>Everything travels over encrypted connections, and only the site’s administrator can see private records. No system is perfect, and if something goes wrong that affects you, we’ll tell you.</p>

<h3>Changes</h3>
<p>The date at the top shows the current version. If we change something significant, artists and venues will hear about it by email first.</p>
</LegalPage>
